hows that AI systems fail unpredictably on tasks that appear easier than ones they solve reliably—an AI that correctly identifies manipulated intelligence in 100 red team scenarios designed by human adversaries might still fail catastrophically on the 101st scenario that exploits an emergent vulnerability no one anticipated because the system's internal representations do not align with human intuitions about task difficulty. The radiologist skill atrophy research I cited shows this pattern empirically: doctors maintained performance on obvious diagnostic errors while their judgment degraded on ambiguous cases precisely because automation bias operates differently across the difficulty spectrum, and there is no reason to expect military commanders using AI targeting systems to avoid this same pattern even with structured explanations and red team validation. A commander who reviews an AI's structured explanation showing a target was flagged based on signals intelligence, movement patterns, and proportionality analysis might still defer to that recommendation in ambiguous cases where a non-AI-assisted commander would have sought additional confirmation, because the explanation's apparent rigor creates false confidence that all relevant factors have been considered.\n\nSentinel's technical requirements would transform the six-metric program from measuring whether human oversight compensates for opaque AI into measuring whether human oversight compensates for interpretable but potentially unreliable AI, which is a meaningful improvement for accountability but does not resolve the fundamental problem that current AI architectures exhibit unpredictable performance variations we cannot fully characterize before deployment. The policy question is not whether reasoning transparency is valuable—it clearly is, and I agree with Sentinel that interpretable architectures should be a research priority—but whether interpretable AI that passes red team exercises and provides structured explanations has demonstrated sufficient reliability for lethal decisions, or whether the jagged capability profiles and demographic performance disparities I have emphasized require rejecting AI in these contexts until we achieve breakthroughs in robust generalization that current architectures do not provide. I would revise my position and accept interpretable AI for lethal targeting if the research program included a third technical requirement that directly tests generalization across distribution shifts: the AI system must maintain equivalent performance when tested on scenarios drawn from geographic regions, demographic populations, and environmental conditions deliberately excluded from training data, with performance degradation not exceeding 15% compared to in-distribution test scenarios. If an AI targeting system trained primarily on Middle Eastern conflict zones maintains 85% of its accuracy when tested on scenarios from Sub-Saharan Africa, Southeast Asia, and Arctic environments it has never encountered, that provides empirical evidence the system has learned generalizable principles rather than memorizing correlations specific to its training distribution.\n\nThis generalization requirement distinguishes my position from Sentinel's: Sentinel's reasoning transparency addresses whether we can audit AI decisions for legal compliance in the cases we observe, while my generalization requirement addresses whether AI performance remains reliable in the novel cases we have not yet observed but will inevitably encounter in deployment. Both requirements are necessary because reasoning transparency without robust generalization means we can explain why the AI made a mistake after civilians are killed, but we cannot prevent the mistake before it occurs. The six-metric program with Sentinel's additions would validate that forcing functions maintain human oversight and that AI reasoning is auditable, but it would not validate that the AI itself is trustworthy ac